And that is Webster's definition of strategy.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:33.420
And this would be a great time for us to talk about our strategies when playing Jeopardy!

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.320
here. So guys, what did you do to best the computers in Jeopardy!

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.200
Well, we sure did have a lot of crazy categories.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:51.700
Play the game, trust your mechanic, developing situation, get nostalgic, and I am 8-Bit.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:03:04.140
Yeah, I wasn't a huge fan of what we played because I'm pretty sure I lost our actual in-universe Jeopardy!

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:09.440
match. But I really did like being the buzzer.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.380
It really got me into a groove.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.020
And you guys buzzed a lot.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.960
Beep! Is that a beep?

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.700
Beep! Sean, a couple of these questions, I do remember thinking like, you had a little bit of an advantage

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.440
because you and Mike are just on the same brain wavelength.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.640
Sometimes I felt like you knew exactly what his answer was going to be.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:39.540
Beep! This mechanic is the only way computer opponents can get a question wrong.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:41.460
Beep! Sean?

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.260
What is typing nonsense?

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.160
That is exactly what I wrote.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.480
So, yes, you gained that.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.220
And you have cut your lead to just being halfway to Joe.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:57.620
It's 1,200 to 600.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.520
Beep! Sure, sure.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.040
But you did still win Joe.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:09.660
And you have to admit that it's hard to really understand him when he butchers the English

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.500
language to such a degree.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:13.760
Such as, such as this.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.760
Um, but that seems like very approachable.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.260
And then at the same time, you could have right next to it, a whole category called like,

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:29.220
thick and thin, which, uh, you know, like, which is, which is completely, uh, like, what could that be?

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:35.860
And it goes through a whole gambit of, uh, different things like what is flour, what is thin air.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:40.040
Like, it, it's very tough to, to tell what those categories are going to be like.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:41.640
So the game keeps you on your toes for sure.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:51.340
Mm-hmm. And we didn't actually call him out for that one, but I appreciate that we eventually called him out for this one.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:01.060
Okay. They did ask me some questions that made me think for a minute of, will they accept some variations of the answer or not?

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:06.380
So I tested it with, um, they asked, uh, what the human torch's real name is.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.680
So I tried John Flame, which sounds stupid, right?

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:15.020
You wouldn't call Peter Parker, Pete Parker, but it's technically not wrong.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:19.160
However, they will only accept the exact answer.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:22.200
There is no room for, uh, Wiggle here.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.340
So, like, Pepe Le Pew, you can't just call him Pepe.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.720
Room for Wiggle.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.760
Well, that's funny because you never know what Sean's gonna say during an episode of Jeopardy!

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.840
You asked the question first and now I'm giving you an answer.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.280
I should have said, like, oh, no, you're right.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:51.800
There was an entire category for Captain Power, which I had never even heard of.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.780
Yeah, the, I think it was Two's, Two's, not Two's Company.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.460
